  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Chilly this morning with frost to high- elevation valleys. \n\n- A brief warm-up with dry conditions and low relative humidities early\n  this week. \n\n- Increasing thunderstorm chances Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday,\n  gusty outflow wind threat. Near-critical fire weather risk in \n  southwest Montana. \n\n- An active, cooler weather pattern with above-normal precipitation\n  probabilities late this week. \n\n\nToday through Tuesday: Optimal radiational cooling under clear \nskies, light winds, and a dry air mass will yield chilly morning \ntemperatures today. High probabilities (70-90%) for frost exist \nin the higher valleys along the Continental Divide and north-\ncentral Idaho, where lows will dip into the upper 20s to lower \n30s. Patchy light frost is also possible in typical cold spots \nwithin lower valleys, including the Stevensville area and the far \nwestern Missoula Valley.\n\nA brief warming and drying trend begins today into Tuesday as \nhigh pressure builds over the Northern Rockies. High temperatures \nwill rebound into the 80s for most valleys, running roughly 10 \ndegrees above seasonal normals by Tuesday. By Tuesday afternoon, \nmid- level moisture increases from the south, initiating scattered\nshowers and isolated thunderstorms across Idaho and Lemhi \ncounties that will track northward. Steep low-level lapse rates \nbeneath high storm bases (~10,000 ft AGL) and a dry sub-cloud \nlayer (inverted-V profile) will favor strong outflow winds. High-\nresolution guidance indicates gusts of 35-50 mph, with a low \nprobability (less than 5%) of localized severe gusts exceeding 60\nmph.\n\nWednesday through Weekend: A well-defined shortwave disturbance \nwill bring widespread showers and thunderstorms Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, with gusty outflow winds remaining a \nhazard, especially across southwest Montana. Elevated to near-\ncritical fire weather conditions are expected across southwest \nMontana ahead of convective initiation. Wetting rain (> 0.10 in) \nprobabilities have increased to 70-90% across much of the \nNorthern Rockies, with a 50-70% chance of accumulations exceeding \n0.25 inches.\n\nShowers, isolated thunderstorms, and elevated winds will persist \nThursday and Friday before surface winds diminish into the \nweekend. Ensemble guidance shows high confidence in a longwave \ntrough remaining anchored over the Pacific Northwest. This pattern\nmaintains southwesterly flow across the Northern Rockies, supporting\nan active regime with above-normal precipitation probabilities \nand daily convective activity.",
